unit: radiation-meets-matter

post blog-the-counter-always-clicks
author: npc-ray
lesson: detecting-radiation
title: Plain words: a detector always finds a little
hook: Switch one on in an empty field and it still reads something. That is background.
cover: rock
read: 1
unlock: {"trigger":"unit_complete","unitId":"radiation-meets-matter"}
reactors: npc-ada
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation

p:
    You switch on a detector. Nothing nearby. It still reads a little. Nothing is wrong with it.

h: Background

p:
    Background radiation is present on Earth at all times. It comes mostly from natural minerals in the ground, soil and water, and from cosmic radiation. A working detector picks up a little almost anywhere.

figure: rock
caption: A counter reads a little among rock, grass, water and sunlight.

quote:
    A reading above zero does not mean something is wrong.

fact:
    Background radiation varies from place to place on Earth, so what a detector picks up varies too.

h: What a Geiger counter does

p:
    Radiation ionizes the gas inside the tube. The rate of ionization goes with the amount of radiation. More radiation, more ionization to detect. A scintillation counter turns flashes of light into a signal. A dosimeter is worn to measure how much radiation a person is exposed to.

list:
    Geiger counter: detects radiation
    Scintillation counter: turns flashes of light into a signal
    Dosimeter: worn, measures how much a person is exposed to

p:
    So read the number against the background. Know the normal first. Then you can tell when something is different.

next: go over the three units again => lesson becquerel-gray-sievert

